// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Centurion Longinus (Background Story)
// ACT: Act III
// CASE: The Final Sacrifice
// CASE ID: crucifixion_site
// SOURCE: NPC 'centurion_background'
// BIBLE REFERENCE: Matthew 8:5-13; Luke 7:1-10; Matthew 27:45-54;
//   Mark 15:33-39; John 19:31-37
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// EVIDENCE UNLOCKED:
//   - evidence_centurion_faith (a Gentile centurion's confession)
//   - evidence_crucifixion_nails (the three nails of crucifixion)
//   - evidence_crucified_with_thieves (the two criminals alongside Jesus)
//   - evidence_sour_wine_sponge (the sour wine offered on a hyssop branch)
//   - evidence_final_words (Jesus's final declaration "It is finished")
//   - evidence_temple_curtain (the veil torn from top to bottom)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED:
//   - Isaiah 53:10-11
//     Gospel: The righteous servant's sacrifice accomplished
//     justification for many. The centurion's confession was the
//     first Gentile acknowledgment of Jesus's divine identity.
//     Insight: The salvation that the centurion glimpsed from the
//     foot of the cross was the fulfillment of every prophetic
//     promise — now witnessed by a Roman officer.
//   - Psalm 34:20
//     Gospel: John 19:36 — "These things happened that the Scripture
//     might be fulfilled: 'Not one of His bones shall be broken.'"
//     Insight: The centurion, who had overseen the breaking of legs
//     for the two thieves, witnessed the divine protection of
//     Christ's bones by the angelic intervention at the tomb.
//   - Zechariah 12:10
//     Gospel: John 19:37 — "They looked on Him whom they had
//     pierced." The centurion's confession echoes the mourning
//     predicted for the ultimate day of salvation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BIBLICAL CONTEXT:
//   Summary: Longinus, a Roman centurion who had once witnessed
//   Jesus heal a servant from a distance, commanded the execution
//   detail at Golgotha and saw the darkness, the earthquake, the
//   opened tombs, and the spear-thrust that confirmed Jesus's death.
//   Significance: His journey from skeptical professional soldier
//   to the man who declared "Truly this was the Son of God" frames
//   the crucifixion through the eyes of an outsider whose earlier
//   brush with Jesus's power made him uniquely positioned to
//   recognize what he was witnessing.
// ============================================================

-> start
=== start ===
They call me Longinus. Centurion of the Augustan cohort. I have served Rome for twenty-three years and seen men die for reasons I could not fathom. Most executions are routine. This one — this one was different from the moment the sky went black.
* [What made it different?] -> different
* [You came to investigate this man first?] -> investigation

=== investigation ===
Yes. Earlier in my career, I had heard reports of a miracle worker from Capernaum — a man who stilled a storm with a word, healed a centurion's servant from a distance, and calmed the fears of those around him. The boy — he was dying of the plague. I sent word. The man never came. But the boy lived. Three days later, the fever broke. I attributed it to medicine. Now I know better.
* [You studied His ways.] -> study
* [The servant's healing.] -> servant_healing

=== servant_healing ===
My servant was a boy — no older than sixteen. The plague had him burning like a furnace. I sent messengers to the Galilean rabbi who had been traveling through our region. The word I received was simple: "Tell him, 'Go your way. He is healed.'" My runners arrived the next morning. The boy was awake. His fever had broken. No physician touched him.
* [You thought it was coincidence.] -> coincidence
* [But you kept the report.] -> keep_record

=== coincidence ===
Of course I thought it was coincidence. Of course I recorded it as medical recovery. A centurion's faith is not measured by miracles — it is measured by discipline, by order, by the ability to follow the chain of command regardless of chaos. I noted the healing but dismissed the cause. Pride blinds us all.
* [What changed your mind?] -> change_mind
* [You were wrong about coincidence.] -> evidence

=== change_mind ===
What changed my mind was watching a man die the death of a king without a king's crown, without a king's court, without a king's army. The darkness that fell at noon. The earthquake that opened tombs. The veil that tore from top to bottom. The centurion's sword finding its mark in the side that bore the weight of the world.
* [You saw all of it.] -> witness
* [And now you believe.] -> faith_confession

=== faith_confession ===
"Truly this was the Son of God." Those were the words I spoke. Not in triumph. Not in victory. But in awe. I had spent twenty-three years learning how men die. On this day, I learned what it means when God dies.
* [The confession was costly.] -> costly
* [What it means.] -> understanding

=== costly ===
My confession cost me my career. My colleagues branded me a traitor. My superiors reprimanded me. The Sanhedrin complained to Rome. They said I had been hypnotized, that I had seen illusions, that any soldier with saltpeter in his water can hallucinate a god. But I saw what I saw.
* [You stood by your testimony.] -> testimony
* [They could not convince you.] -> conviction

=== testimony ===
I stood by my testimony because it was not based on visions or hallucinations. It was based on observation. Blood and water from the spear wound. The way His body bowed without resistance. The way the earth trembled beneath His feet. These were not the signs of a dying man. These were the signs of divinity made manifest in human flesh.
* [Evidence, not faith.] -> evidence_based
* [The evidence was overwhelming.] -> overwhelming

=== evidence_based ===
Evidence does not require faith — evidence requires documentation. I have recorded everything: the darkness from the sixth to the ninth hour, the earthquake, the rocks splitting, the tombs opening, the veil tearing. I have noted the soldiers' testimonies, the crowd's reactions, and the inexplicable phenomena that accompanied His final hours.
* [The documentation was thorough.] -> thorough_docs
* [What the records showed.] -> divine_evidence

=== thorough_docs ===
My documentation was thorough because the events demanded explanation. A man who had never raised a hand in violence, who had healed the sick without charge, who had welcomed children and forgave sinners — this man did not die like other men. The records show that when He died, creation itself responded.
* [The creation responded.] -> creation_responds
* [The evidence was divine.] -> overwhelming

=== creation_responds ===
The earth shook. The rocks split. The tombs opened. The veil tore. The sky grew dark. These were not the actions of a mortal man. These were the responses of creation to the presence of the divine. When the Creator of heaven and earth drew His final breath, the creation recognized its Maker.
* [And you were there.] -> witness
* [The evidence was divine.] -> overwhelming

=== overwhelming ===
The evidence was overwhelming. The centurion who had seen a thousand executions witnessed something unprecedented — a death that opened graves, tore curtains from top to bottom, and caused a soldier's sword to find its mark in a side that had never known violence. This was not execution. This was coronation.
* [The crown of thorns.] -> crown_thorns
* [The cross was His throne.] -> cross_throne

=== crown_thorns ===
He wore a crown of thorns — not gold, not jewels, not the diadems of kings. A crown of suffering, of sacrifice, of love that gives itself completely. And when He died, it was not defeat. It was the moment when every prophecy — every type, every shadow, every promise — found its fulfillment in a single, perfect act.
* [The cross was His throne.] -> cross_throne
* [The moment of coronation.] -> moment_of_coronation

=== cross_throne ===
The cross was His throne. Not of conquest, but of service. Not of dominion, but of sacrifice. Not of power over others, but of love that lays itself down. The same voice that had calmed storms and healed the sick now spoke the words that would echo through eternity: "It is finished."
* [What did those words mean?] -> it_is_finished
* [The completion of the work.] -> completion

=== it_is_finished ===
"Tetelestai." The account is rendered complete. The debt is paid in full. The sacrifice is offered. The covenant is sealed. The kingdom is inaugurated. When He spoke those words, the centurion felt the weight of history shifting beneath his sandals — a Roman legionnaire had just witnessed the moment when eternity intersected with time.
* [The debt was paid.] -> completion
* [The covenant was sealed.] -> completion

=== completion ===
The debt was paid. The covenant was sealed. The kingdom was inaugurated. The sacrifice was complete. And I — a centurion who had sworn allegiance to Caesar but found his true loyalty in the God who died on a cross — knew that I had seen something that would define the rest of my days.
