* [You kept investigating.] -> investigation_continued
* [You became a different kind of soldier.] -> different_soldier

=== investigation_continued ===
I kept investigating. Three days later, the reports came in from the tomb. The guards had been bribed by the chief priests. The eyewitnesses claimed to have seen Him walking. I dismissed the reports as I had dismissed the resurrection claims — until I met one of those eyewitnesses in the marketplace.
* [The eyewitness met you.] -> eyewitness_meet
* [Your skepticism was tested.] -> tested_belief

=== eyewitness_meet ===
She was an older woman — weathered hands, sun-bronzed skin, eyes that held a depth I could not fathom. She spoke of Mary Magdalene. Of empty tombs. Of voices calling from the garden. Of a man who had died and was alive again. When she finished, she simply said: "You saw the Son of God die. I saw the Son of God rise. Which witness will you believe?"
* [The question was simple.] -> tested_belief
* [The answer was certain.] -> faith_final

=== tested_belief ===
My skepticism was tested by evidence I could not explain. The tomb was empty. The witnesses were consistent. The transformation was inexplicable. Men who had hidden behind locked doors now faced execution for their claims. Women who risked social ostracism testified to what they had seen.
* [The evidence accumulated.] -> accumulating_evidence
* [You made your choice.] -> faith_final

=== accumulating_evidence ===
The evidence accumulated like sand in an hourglass — each grain a testimony, each hour a new witness, each day another reason to believe that the man who had died on the cross was indeed the Son of God. I kept my records. I kept my rank. But I surrendered my unbelief.
* [The records were updated.] -> final_record
* [Your faith was complete.] -> faith_final

=== final_record ===
My updated records read: "Observed the execution of Jesus of Nazareth on the 14th of Nisan. Documented the supernatural phenomena accompanying His death. Investigated the resurrection claims and found them substantiated by eyewitness testimony, empty tomb verification, and transformed lives. Converted to the faith I once sought to destroy."
* [The conversion was complete.] -> faith_final
* [The record speaks for itself.] -> faith_final

=== faith_final ===
My faith was complete. Not because I wanted it to be. Not because it was easy. But because the evidence was overwhelming, the testimony was consistent, and the love I had witnessed on the cross was a love that transcended death itself. I had seen the Son of God die. Now I believed He was the Son of God risen.
* [The centurion's confession endures.] -> closing

=== different ===
My servant's healing. The words He spoke from the cross. The darkness that fell at noon. The way the centurion's sword found its mark in the side that bore the weight of the world. These were not the signs of a dying man. These were the signs of divinity made manifest in human flesh.
* [You believed.] -> faith_confession
* [The evidence was divine.] -> overwhelming

=== witness ===
I was there. I saw the darkness fall. I saw the earthquake. I saw the rocks split. I saw the centurion's soldiers fall to their faces, crying, "Truly this was the Son of God!" And I — I who had studied the ways of men, who had calculated the odds of survival, who had measured courage in inches and inches alone — I knew I had witnessed something beyond measure.
* [The confession was public.] -> faith_confession
* [What you recorded.] -> thorough_docs

=== moment_of_coronation ===
The moment of coronation. When He bowed His head and said, "It is finished," the universe recognized its Creator. When the sword pierced His side, the fountain of grace was opened for all who would come. And I — I who had crucified the King — found myself kneeling before the King of kings.
* [The crown of thorns.] -> crown_thorns
* [The fountain of grace.] -> completion

=== different_soldier ===
I became a different kind of soldier. Not a soldier of Rome, but a soldier of Christ. Not a servant of Caesar, but a servant of the Gospel. The centurion's sword that had pierced the side of the Savior now carved the name of Jesus into my heart. I served a different empire now — one that conquers not by force, but by love.
* [The confession was public.] -> faith_confession
* [The sword became a symbol.] -> faith_final

=== closing ===
They call me Longinus, centurion of the Augustan cohort. I have served Rome for twenty-three years and witnessed the most extraordinary event in human history. I have seen the darkness fall. I have seen the earth shake. I have seen the veil tear from top to bottom. And I have seen the Son of God die on a cross, and I have believed.
* [The confession endures.] -> final_record
-> DONE
